1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,920
Claiming got cheap.

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:03,340
Checking did not.

3
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:09,460
Here is a reference: two authors, a year,
a title, a journal and some page numbers.

4
00:00:10,060 --> 00:00:12,260
It looks like every other reference
you have read.

5
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:14,140
I made it up for this video.

6
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:17,342
A chatbot can write
one like it in under a second,

7
00:00:17,342 --> 00:00:20,180
and nothing on the page
tells you whether the work exists.

8
00:00:20,780 --> 00:00:23,260
To find out,
someone has to go and look for it.

9
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:27,166
In 2023, William Walters and Esther Wilder

10
00:00:27,166 --> 00:00:30,752
had ChatGPT write
eighty-four short literature reviews,

11
00:00:30,752 --> 00:00:35,420
and then they searched for every one of
the six hundred and thirty-six references.

12
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:40,920
Fifty-five percent of the references
from GPT-3.5 did not exist.

13
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:44,080
For GPT-4, the share was eighteen percent.

14
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:48,176
Many of the real ones
were wrong in their details too:

15
00:00:48,176 --> 00:00:52,400
forty-three percent for GPT-3.5
and twenty-four percent for GPT-4.

16
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,680
On the page,
the invented and the real look the same.

17
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:57,740
Only the search told them apart.

18
00:00:58,940 --> 00:01:01,020
Science already pays people to check.

19
00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:03,073
By reviewers' own reports,

20
00:01:03,073 --> 00:01:06,540
one peer review takes about six hours
of a skilled person's time.

21
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:11,940
Balazs Aczel and colleagues estimated
about twenty-two million reviews in 2020.

22
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:14,927
Six hours each comes
to over a hundred million hours:

23
00:01:14,927 --> 00:01:17,760
about fifteen thousand years of work,
spent in one year.

24
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:21,091
It is an estimate built
from approximate rates,

25
00:01:21,091 --> 00:01:23,760
and the authors expect
the true figure to be higher.

26
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:27,220
Meanwhile, the unchecked claim
does not wait.

27
00:01:27,540 --> 00:01:31,371
Soroush Vosoughi, Deb Roy
and Sinan Aral followed

28
00:01:31,371 --> 00:01:34,202
about a hundred and twenty-six thousand

29
00:01:34,202 --> 00:01:37,700
rumor cascades on Twitter,
from 2006 to 2017.

30
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:41,471
False stories were seventy percent
more likely to be retweeted,

31
00:01:41,471 --> 00:01:44,860
and the truth took about six times as long
to reach fifteen hundred people.

32
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:48,011
Bots sped up true and false news
at the same rate,

33
00:01:48,011 --> 00:01:50,040
which points to people as the difference.

34
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:52,960
That study measures spread, not cost.

35
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:55,096
The old line that refuting a claim

36
00:01:55,096 --> 00:01:57,820
takes ten times the effort
of making it is an adage.

37
00:01:58,140 --> 00:02:00,160
Nobody has measured that ratio.

38
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:04,469
Claims got cheap,
and a check still costs hours,

39
00:02:04,469 --> 00:02:07,060
so the gap can only close
from the checking side.

40
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:11,340
When the data travel with the claim,
a check becomes a rerun.

41
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:14,394
Tom Hardwicke and colleagues tried
to reproduce the numbers

42
00:02:14,394 --> 00:02:17,020
in thirty-five psychology papers
that shared their data.

43
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:20,075
Where the numbers came out straight away,
a check took

44
00:02:20,075 --> 00:02:22,380
about two to four person hours,
by their estimate.

45
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:25,113
Where they had to ask
the authors for help,

46
00:02:25,113 --> 00:02:27,960
it took five to twenty-five,
and weeks of waiting.

47
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:30,434
Thirteen of the thirty-five still

48
00:02:30,434 --> 00:02:33,020
had at least one number
nobody could reproduce.

49
00:02:33,340 --> 00:02:36,429
None of those misses clearly
changed a paper's conclusion,

50
00:02:36,429 --> 00:02:38,260
and the rerun is how anyone knows that.

51
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:40,600
Two things to keep.

52
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:43,800
How a claim looks tells you nothing
about whether it holds.

53
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:46,340
And the cheapest check
is the one that ships

54
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:48,280
with the claim, so anyone can rerun it.

55
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:51,520
The voice you heard
is a synthesized version of mine.

56
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:54,760
Every number on screen links
to its source under the video.
